#X obj 800 400 + 1;
#X obj 20 420 list prepend;
#X obj 20 470 text set \$0-log;
#X obj 1000 190 r \$0-openlog;
#X msg 1000 230 click;
#X obj 1000 290 text define \$0-log;
#X text 20 560 in-l and in-r are filtered out: they arrive ten times a second and say nothing about what the SCREEN is doing. Everything else on the bus -- parameters \, modals \, alerts \, boot stages -- is exactly the sequence worth reading back., f 46;
#X text 420 560 The trigger reads right to left: fetch the frame number \, take the next line number \, then push the content through. Both have to be in place before text set sees the line \, because both are cold inlets., f 46;
#X text 820 560 Writing to a line past the end of the buffer APPENDS -- verified in 0.49 rather than assumed \, which is how the log grows without anything having to track its length., f 46;
#X text 820 700 Stamped with the FRAME number \, not a clock \, so gaps read directly as tenths of a second and line up with what the screen was showing., f 46;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 2 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 2 6 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 1;
#X connect 6 0 10 1;
#X connect 4 1 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 1 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 7 1;
#X connect 8 0 11 1;
#X connect 4 0 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 11 0;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X connect 13 0 14 0;
#X restore 1140 620 pd log;
#X text 40 680 u_mother-stub -- impersonates mother.pd when Cut It runs on the Mac \, where mother.pd does not exist \, AND is the dev panel. main-dev.pd instantiates it \; main.pd does not \, so the device never sees it., f 58;
#X text 40 775 THE ONE SANCTIONED EXCEPTION to the reserved-name rule C-2 -- it deliberately sends on names owned by mother.pd. That is its entire purpose., f 58;
#X text 40 860 NO CORDS ON THE PANEL \, ON PURPOSE. Every control above binds by its iemgui SEND name -- knob1 goes straight to knob1 -- and every readout by its RECEIVE name. That is what lets the whole face render on main-dev.pd through graph-on-parent \, which draws iemguis and atom boxes and nothing else. See ref/conventions.md \, GUI binding., f 58;
#X text 640 680 The eight black strips are the 128x64 screen \, one per 8-pixel row \, keyed by the y coordinate of each drawing command. They are cnv objects and the text is their LABEL \, which is why pd oled-decode has to join each row into a single symbol first., f 58;
#X text 640 800 Layout follows the real front panel: aux \, knobs 1-4 \, screen \, selector encoder \, volume \, keys underneath. Anything below this line is plumbing and is invisible on main-dev.pd., f 58;
#X text 1240 680 The bench row is the Phase 3 acceptance run. The screen log is the answer to a boot sequence that finishes before you can look at it: click open-screen-log and read what happened., f 58;
#X obj 40 530 bng 24 250 50 0 empty clock beat 0 -14 0 11 -262144 -1 -1;
#X floatatom 140 530 5 0 0 1 tempo-bus tempo -;
#X floatatom 280 530 3 0 0 1 aux-LED led -;
#X symbolatom 420 530 11 0 0 0 - \$0-ledcol -;
#X obj 580 530 bng 24 250 50 0 \$0-auxtap empty aux-tap 0 -14 0 11 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 660 530 bng 24 250 50 0 \$0-b-start empty start 0 -14 0 11 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 740 530 bng 24 250 50 0 \$0-b-stop empty stop 0 -14 0 11 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 820 530 bng 24 250 50 0 \$0-b-panic empty panic 0 -14 0 11 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 920 530 bng 24 250 50 0 \$0-b-thi empty bpm-hi 0 -14 0 11 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 1000 530 bng 24 250 50 0 \$0-b-tlo empty bpm-lo 0 -14 0 11 -262144 -1 -1;
#N canvas 40 40 1400 1120 bench5 0;
#X text 20 20 The Phase 5 row on the panel above. The tempo control is knob 1 itself -- it already sends knob1 \, m_organelle turns that into og-knob-1 and u_map decides it means tempo -- so there is nothing here for it. What IS here is everything a knob cannot do., f 58;
#X obj 20 150 r \$0-auxtap;
#X obj 20 190 t b b;
#X msg 20 240 1;
#X obj 20 320 s aux;
#X obj 180 190 del 120;
#X msg 180 240 0;
#X obj 460 150 r \$0-b-start;
#X obj 460 190 s start;
#X obj 620 150 r \$0-b-stop;
#X obj 620 190 s stop;
#X obj 780 150 r \$0-b-panic;
#X obj 780 190 s panic;
#X obj 960 150 r \$0-b-thi;
#X msg 960 190 5000;
#X obj 960 240 s tempo;
#X obj 1120 150 r \$0-b-tlo;
#X msg 1120 190 0;
#X obj 1120 240 s tempo;
#X text 20 400 THE AUX TAP IS A PRESS AND A RELEASE \, 120 ms apart \, because that is what mother sends and m_organelle only acts on the 1. The panel's aux toggle works too \, but it takes two clicks to make one press \, which is a poor way to test a transport., f 58;
#X text 20 530 bpm-hi and bpm-lo are the out-of-range pair: 5000 and 0 \, against a legal range of 5 to 600. Both must clamp -- to 600 and to 5 -- and each must raise its OWN alert. Press the same one twice and the second must be silent \, because the VALUE did not change., f 58;
#X text 20 660 start \, stop and panic are here because nothing else on the Mac can send them: the aux button is the only physical route to the transport and there is no aux button on a laptop., f 58;
#X obj 20 760 r led;
#X obj 20 800 select 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7;
#X msg 20 850 symbol off;
#X msg 140 850 symbol red;
#X msg 260 850 symbol yellow;
#X msg 400 850 symbol green;
#X msg 540 850 symbol cyan;
#X msg 680 850 symbol dark-blue;
#X msg 820 850 symbol pink;
#X msg 960 850 symbol white;
#X obj 20 950 s \$0-ledcol;
#X text 20 1160 TWO BPM READOUTS \, AND THEY ARE MEANT TO DISAGREE. tempo-bus on the panel is the bus -- what was REQUESTED -- so it reads 5000 when you press bpm-hi. The OLED row shows what u_tempo actually did with it \, which is 600. Seeing the request and the clamp side by side is the point \; u_tempo deliberately never re-emits the clamped value \, because writing back to a bus it also listens to is a loop., f 58;
#X text 20 1010 THE PANEL SHOWS THE COLOUR AS A WORD \, not as a radio cell you have to count. mother receives a NUMBER on led -- that is what the floatatom beside it shows \, faithfully -- and this turns the same number into the name g_led meant by it. Ten seconds a step is not long enough to count boxes., f 58;
